\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces HealthGAN (WGAN-GP) architecture and training configuration, as fixed in \texttt {wgan\_for\_mac.py}.}}{23}{table.caption.20}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Fidelity of the three retained HealthGAN synthetic draws against the real test partition (lower is better on both measures). The spread across draws is negligible; draw $0$ was selected as representative.}}{24}{table.caption.21}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces PATE-GAN architecture, training, and privacy configuration used in this thesis.}}{27}{table.caption.22}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Comparative summary of the five synthesisers. All generators are trained on the same $57{,}214$-record training partition of the Diabetes 130-US Hospitals benchmark and supply synthetic outputs whose sizes are matched to the partitions used in the downstream evaluation pipeline of Chapter~\ref {ch:results}. Privacy budgets are reported as target $(\varepsilon , \delta )$ values; the realised $\varepsilon $ for Pythia-1B-DP is reported in \S \ref {sec:results:rq3:llm-toggle}.}}{32}{table.caption.23}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Distinct data-characteristic dimension captured by each metric in the evaluation suite, and the primary blind spot that motivates reporting it alongside complementary metrics in the same branch. The listed metrics correspond to the comprehensive summary view of Section~\ref {sec:eval:aggregate:table}.}}{43}{table.caption.45}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.2}{\ignorespaces Mean Kolmogorov--Smirnov statistic and mean pairwise correlation difference per generator (lower is better on both). KS is averaged over all compared columns; PCD is the mean absolute difference between the real and synthetic correlation matrices. The best non-trivial value in each column is shown in bold.}}{47}{table.caption.50}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.3}{\ignorespaces Train-on-Synthetic, Test-on-Real (TSTR) utility for all five generators and the Train-on-Real (TRTR) reference, evaluated on the real held-out split ($n = 14{,}304$) with \texttt {readmitted} as the target. The best synthetic AUC-ROC per classifier block is shown in bold.}}{50}{table.caption.56}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.4}{\ignorespaces Cox proportional-hazards confidence-interval overlap per generator: the number of covariates whose synthetic $95\%$ hazard-ratio interval overlaps the real interval, out of those fitted (higher is better). HealthGAN is fitted on twelve covariates because one zero-variance column was dropped.}}{53}{table.caption.62}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.5}{\ignorespaces TSTR utility under the discarded three-bin age encoding versus the retained ten-bin encoding. Test set: real held-out split ($n = 14{,}304$); target: \texttt {readmitted}; classifier: Gradient Boosting. $\Delta $ reports the change from three-bin to ten-bin.}}{54}{table.caption.64}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.6}{\ignorespaces Median nearest-neighbour distances and distance ratio per generator. The real-to-real median nearest-neighbour distance is $2.019$ for reference. Larger distances and $\text {NNDR} \geq 1$ indicate lower proximity-based disclosure risk.}}{55}{table.caption.68}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.7}{\ignorespaces Nearest-neighbour adversarial accuracy per generator. Train and test adversarial accuracy near $0.5$ indicates good resemblance; privacy loss (test $-$ train) near $0.0$ indicates no memorisation.}}{56}{table.caption.69}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.8}{\ignorespaces Membership-inference attack results per generator. AUC near $0.5$ and advantage near $0.0$ indicate that the adversary cannot distinguish training members from non-members.}}{56}{table.caption.70}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.9}{\ignorespaces Attribute-inference advantage per generator and attribute (positive values indicate leakage above the marginal baseline). The final column is the per-generator maximum advantage.}}{57}{table.caption.71}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.10}{\ignorespaces Outlier versus non-outlier linkability per generator (percentage of real records with a synthetic neighbour within radius $2.636$). Low outlier linkability indicates that the generator does not concentrate mass around vulnerable records; a positive gap indicates coverage of common rather than extreme patterns.}}{58}{table.caption.72}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.11}{\ignorespaces Comprehensive summary of the eleven headline metrics across the five generators and the real (TRTR) reference. Arrows indicate the preferred direction; ($\approx 0.5$) indicates that values closest to $0.5$ are best. Bold marks the best synthetic value on each utility row. PATE-GAN's nearest-neighbour distance and ratio are degenerate (off-manifold output) and should be read with Section~\ref {sec:results:rq2:privacy}.}}{61}{table.caption.76}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.12}{\ignorespaces Within-family differential-privacy transition for the GAN family (HealthGAN $\to $ PATE-GAN). $\Delta $ is the private value minus the non-private value; on a utility row a negative $\Delta $ denotes a loss.}}{63}{table.caption.78}%
\contentsline {table}{\numberline {4.13}{\ignorespaces Within-family differential-privacy transition for the LLM family (Pythia-1B $\to $ Pythia-1B-DP), a clean toggle on a shared backbone at achieved $\varepsilon = 4.998$. $\Delta $ is the private value minus the non-private value.}}{64}{table.caption.79}%
